-- CISSP Domain 4 (Communication and Network Security) expansion: objective 4.1 (secure network architecture and protocols - TCP three-way handshake, TCP/IP-vs-OSI layer mapping, IKE Phase 1/2, WPA2-Enterprise vs WPA2-Personal, VLAN hopping/double tagging, SNMPv3, iSCSI CHAP authentication, SDN northbound/southbound APIs; OSI-layer-order, TCP-vs-UDP, generic segmentation benefit, zero trust, generic secure-protocol-replacement list, WPA3 SAE, VLAN-segmentation blast-radius benefit, iSCSI-on-flat-network risk, and SDN control/data-plane separation basics intentionally NOT restated, see ci-f-032/033/035/037/038/097/099 and ci-q-022/025/026/027/090/091/097/099/100/101/102), 4.2 (secure network components - firewall generations/proxy and circuit-level gateways, screened subnet + bastion host, NIDS vs HIDS, inline vs passive IDS/IPS deployment, false-positive/false-negative tuning, anomaly-based vs signature-based detection, honeypot, WAF, NAC posture checks, UTM, rule-base implicit-deny/ordering; packet-filter-vs-stateful firewall, IPsec AH/ESP and transport/tunnel mode, NGFW one-line summary, basic IDS-vs-IPS, and signature-detection basics intentionally NOT restated, see ci-f-034/036/093/094/095/096 and ci-q-023/024/092/093/094/095/096), 4.3 (secure communication channels - IPsec vs SSL/TLS VPN, full-tunnel definition, site-to-site vs remote-access VPN, ZTNA vs traditional VPN, RDP NLA and internet-exposure risk, SIP signaling vs SRTP media, VDI, always-on VPN, NAC posture gating remote access, VoIP toll fraud; SRTP-for-VoIP-eavesdropping and split-tunneling-risk basics intentionally NOT restated, see ci-f-098 and ci-q-098/103).

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 4.1 core (ci-f-1100..1105)
('ci-f-1100','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$What are the three steps of the TCP three-way handshake, and what does each segment's flag indicate?$$,$$SYN (client requests a connection, syncing its initial sequence number), SYN-ACK (server acknowledges and syncs its own sequence number), ACK (client acknowledges the server's sequence number) - only after all three complete is the connection considered established, letting a stateful firewall track it as such.$$),
('ci-f-1101','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$How does the four-layer TCP/IP model map onto the seven-layer OSI model?$$,$$TCP/IP's Application layer covers OSI's Application, Presentation, and Session layers; TCP/IP's Transport layer maps to OSI's Transport layer; TCP/IP's Internet layer maps to OSI's Network layer; TCP/IP's Network Access (Link) layer covers OSI's Data Link and Physical layers. OSI is a conceptual teaching model; TCP/IP is the model actually implemented on the internet.$$),
('ci-f-1102','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the difference between IKE Phase 1 and Phase 2 when establishing an IPsec VPN?$$,$$Phase 1 establishes a secure, authenticated management channel (an ISAKMP/IKE SA) between the two peers, negotiating how they will protect further negotiation - it can run in Main Mode (more exchanges, hides identities) or the faster but less protected Aggressive Mode. Phase 2 then uses that protected channel to negotiate the actual IPsec SAs (the AH/ESP parameters) that will protect application data.$$),
('ci-f-1103','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$How does a VLAN hopping double-tagging attack work, and what stops it?$$,$$An attacker on a trunk-accessible port sends a frame with two stacked 802.1Q tags; the first switch strips the outer tag (matching the native VLAN) and forwards the frame, exposing the inner tag to reach a VLAN the attacker shouldn't access. It's prevented by never using the native/default VLAN for user traffic and explicitly tagging or disabling the native VLAN on trunk ports.$$),
('ci-f-1104','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the security difference between WPA2-Enterprise and WPA2-Personal (WPA2-PSK)?$$,$$WPA2-Personal authenticates every device with one shared pre-shared key (PSK), so anyone who learns the passphrase - or leaves the organization - requires a network-wide key change to fully revoke. WPA2-Enterprise instead authenticates each user or device individually against a RADIUS server via 802.1X/EAP, enabling per-user credentials, centralized revocation, and audit logging.$$),
('ci-f-1105','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the difference between an SDN controller's northbound and southbound APIs?$$,$$The southbound API (e.g., OpenFlow) lets the centralized controller push forwarding instructions down to physical/virtual switches - the data plane. The northbound API lets administrators and orchestration/security applications above the controller (e.g., automated policy or threat-response tools) request network changes without touching individual devices directly.$$),
-- 4.1 acronym (ci-a-370..371)
('ci-a-370','cissp','acronym',4,'4.1',$$IKE$$,$$Internet Key Exchange - the protocol IPsec uses to negotiate and establish security associations, running in two phases: Phase 1 builds a protected management channel, Phase 2 negotiates the actual AH/ESP parameters.$$),
('ci-a-371','cissp','acronym',4,'4.1',$$EAP$$,$$Extensible Authentication Protocol - a framework (not a single method) supporting multiple authentication mechanisms (EAP-TLS, PEAP, EAP-TTLS) used with 802.1X for WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise wireless and wired port authentication.$$),
-- 4.1 feynman (ci-y-230)
('ci-y-230','cissp','feynman',4,'4.1',$$Explain why segmenting IoT devices onto their own VLAN does not fully protect them from a determined attacker who already has access to a trunk port on the switch.$$,$$Model answer: VLAN segmentation relies on switches correctly enforcing which VLAN each frame belongs to based on 802.1Q tags, but a double-tagging VLAN hopping attack exploits how trunk ports handle the native VLAN: if an attacker can inject a frame with two stacked tags from a port that carries the native VLAN untagged, the first switch strips the outer (native) tag and forwards the frame with the inner tag intact, letting it cross into a VLAN the attacker was never authorized to reach - all without ever touching a router or an inter-VLAN ACL. Segmentation is a real control, but it depends on switch configuration hygiene (never using the native VLAN for user traffic, explicitly tagging or disabling it on trunks) to actually hold the boundary it promises; a segmentation diagram alone doesn't guarantee the boundary is enforced.$$),
-- 4.2 core (ci-f-1106..1113)
('ci-f-1106','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$How do packet-filtering, circuit-level gateway, and application-level (proxy) firewalls differ in what they inspect?$$,$$A packet-filtering firewall inspects only header fields (source/destination IP, port, protocol) per packet in isolation. A circuit-level gateway (e.g., SOCKS) verifies that a session's handshake is legitimate without inspecting the payload, then passes traffic through for the life of that session. An application-level (proxy) firewall terminates the connection itself, fully inspects and can modify the actual application-layer content (e.g., HTTP requests) before relaying a new connection to the destination.$$),
('ci-f-1107','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is a screened subnet (DMZ), and why does a bastion host sit inside it?$$,$$A screened subnet is a buffer network placed between two firewalls (or one firewall with a third interface) that hosts public-facing services like web or mail servers, so that if one of those servers is compromised, the attacker still faces another firewall before reaching the internal network. A bastion host is a hardened, minimally-configured server placed in that exposed zone specifically because it is expected to face direct attack.$$),
('ci-f-1108','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is the difference between a network-based IDS (NIDS) and a host-based IDS (HIDS)?$$,$$A NIDS monitors traffic crossing a network segment (via a tap or mirrored/SPAN port), seeing activity across many hosts but losing visibility once traffic is encrypted end-to-end. A HIDS runs as an agent on an individual host, monitoring local logs, file integrity, and system calls - it can see what happens after traffic decrypts on that host, but only for that one host.$$),
('ci-f-1109','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is the trade-off between deploying detection inline (as an IPS) versus passively out-of-band (as an IDS) via a tap or SPAN port?$$,$$Inline deployment sits directly in the traffic path, letting it actively block malicious packets in real time, but it adds latency and becomes a potential single point of failure or bottleneck. Out-of-band deployment via a tap or mirrored port only receives a copy of the traffic, so it can alert but never block, and it adds no latency or failure risk to the live path.$$),
('ci-f-1110','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is the tuning trade-off between an IDS/IPS's false-positive rate and its false-negative rate?$$,$$A false positive is legitimate traffic wrongly flagged as malicious; too many overwhelm analysts with noise and cause alert fatigue, risking real alerts being ignored (or the IPS blocking valid traffic). A false negative is malicious traffic that goes undetected; making detection more sensitive to eliminate false negatives typically raises false positives, so tuning is a continuous balance rather than a one-time fix.$$),
('ci-f-1111','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$How does anomaly-based (behavioral) detection differ from signature-based detection, including its main trade-off?$$,$$Signature-based detection matches traffic against known attack patterns - accurate for known threats but blind to novel/zero-day attacks with no matching signature yet. Anomaly-based detection instead builds a baseline of normal behavior and flags significant deviations, letting it catch previously unseen attacks, at the cost of a generally higher false-positive rate since legitimate but unusual activity can also trigger it.$$),
('ci-f-1112','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is a honeypot (or honeynet), and what two purposes does it serve?$$,$$A honeypot is a decoy system deliberately made to look like a legitimate, vulnerable target, isolated from production systems. It serves detection (any interaction with it is inherently suspicious, since no legitimate user has a reason to touch it) and intelligence gathering (observing attacker tools and techniques in a contained environment) - a honeynet is simply a network of multiple honeypots.$$),
('ci-f-1113','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$Why do firewall rule bases end with an implicit/explicit "deny all" rule, and why does rule order matter?$$,$$A default-deny final rule ensures any traffic not explicitly permitted is blocked, following least privilege rather than trusting anything not specifically forbidden. Rule order matters because most firewalls evaluate rules top-down and stop at the first match, so a broad permissive rule placed above a more specific restrictive rule will silently override it - specific rules generally belong above general ones.$$),
-- 4.2 acronym (ci-a-372..373)
('ci-a-372','cissp','acronym',4,'4.2',$$NGFW$$,$$Next-Generation Firewall - a firewall that adds application awareness, deep packet inspection, user identity, and integrated intrusion prevention/threat intelligence on top of traditional stateful packet filtering.$$),
('ci-a-373','cissp','acronym',4,'4.2',$$WAF$$,$$Web Application Firewall - an application-layer firewall placed in front of web applications to inspect HTTP(S) requests and block attacks like SQL injection and cross-site scripting that a network-layer firewall, which only sees ports and IPs, cannot recognize.$$),
-- 4.2 feynman (ci-y-231..232)
('ci-y-231','cissp','feynman',4,'4.2',$$Explain why a next-generation firewall (NGFW) can block a malicious application tunneling its traffic over port 443, when a classic port-based firewall would let it through.$$,$$Model answer: A classic packet-filtering or even stateful firewall makes its allow/deny decision almost entirely on port and protocol - if port 443 is open for HTTPS, any traffic riding on port 443 typically passes, regardless of what it actually is. Attackers exploit this by tunneling malicious command-and-control or exfiltration traffic over 443 disguised as ordinary web traffic. An NGFW instead performs deep packet inspection and application-layer identification, fingerprinting the actual application protocol and content signature riding inside the connection rather than trusting the port number alone - so it can recognize that the traffic on port 443 isn't really HTTPS to a legitimate site, or can decrypt/inspect TLS via a trusted proxy, and block it by application identity and behavior instead of by port.$$),
('ci-y-232','cissp','feynman',4,'4.2',$$Explain why aggressively tuning an IDS to minimize false positives can quietly increase the risk of a real breach going undetected.$$,$$Model answer: False positives and false negatives sit on the same detection dial in tension with each other for most tuning knobs (e.g., anomaly-detection sensitivity thresholds). Turning detection down to stop flagging benign-but-unusual activity as malicious - reducing analyst noise and alert fatigue - also raises the bar an actual attack has to clear before it's flagged at all, meaning genuinely malicious activity that resembles normal traffic more closely now slips through as a false negative. The fix isn't picking one extreme; it's tuning thresholds against a specific environment's real baseline, using layered detection (signature plus anomaly plus log correlation) so a miss in one layer doesn't mean a miss everywhere, and periodically re-tuning as both the baseline and the threat landscape shift.$$),
-- 4.3 core (ci-f-1114..1124)
('ci-f-1114','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$What is the practical difference between an IPsec VPN and an SSL/TLS VPN for remote access?$$,$$An IPsec VPN operates at the network layer, typically requiring a dedicated client and granting the connected device broad network-level access once the tunnel is up. An SSL/TLS VPN operates at a higher layer and can be clientless, delivered through a standard web browser to a specific application or portal, making it easier to deploy for unmanaged devices but often limited to reaching only the specific resources the portal exposes.$$),
('ci-f-1115','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$What is the difference between a full-tunnel and a split-tunnel VPN configuration?$$,$$In a full tunnel, all of a remote user's traffic, including ordinary internet browsing, routes through the corporate VPN and its security stack, maximizing visibility and control at the cost of bandwidth and latency. In a split tunnel, only traffic destined for the corporate network goes through the VPN while other internet traffic exits directly from the local connection, improving performance but leaving that other traffic entirely outside corporate inspection.$$),
('ci-f-1116','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$What is the difference between a site-to-site VPN and a remote-access (client-to-site) VPN?$$,$$A site-to-site VPN is an always-up tunnel between two network gateways (e.g., two office firewalls), transparently connecting entire networks without any end-user client software. A remote-access VPN connects an individual user's device to the corporate network on demand, typically requiring client software (or a browser for SSL VPN) and per-user authentication.$$),
('ci-f-1117','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$How does Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA) differ from a traditional VPN's security model?$$,$$A traditional VPN authenticates once and then grants the device broad network-level access, implicitly trusting anything reachable on that network. ZTNA instead authenticates and authorizes every individual application request, brokering access to one specific application at a time based on continuously evaluated identity and device context, without ever placing the remote device directly on the internal network.$$),
('ci-f-1118','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$What does Network Level Authentication (NLA) add to Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)?$$,$$NLA requires a user to authenticate before a full remote desktop session is established, rather than presenting a login screen only after the session is already open. This cuts resource exhaustion from unauthenticated connection attempts and reduces exposure to pre-authentication vulnerabilities in the RDP session stack itself.$$),
('ci-f-1119','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$Why is exposing RDP directly to the internet (without a VPN or gateway in front of it) considered high risk?$$,$$Internet-facing RDP is a heavily and continuously scanned target for credential-stuffing, brute-force login attempts, and exploitation of RDP-stack vulnerabilities, and it has been a leading initial-access vector for ransomware operators. Best practice places RDP behind a VPN, a Remote Desktop Gateway, or a jump host with MFA, rather than exposing port 3389 to the open internet.$$),
('ci-f-1120','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$In VoIP, what is the difference between what SIP protects and what SRTP protects, and why do both need securing?$$,$$SIP (Session Initiation Protocol) handles call setup, signaling, and teardown - who is calling whom - and should be encrypted with TLS (SIPS) to prevent call metadata tampering or eavesdropping on caller/callee information. SRTP separately encrypts the actual voice media stream. Securing only one leaves the other exposed: SIPS alone still lets someone capture the actual conversation audio, while SRTP alone still lets someone see and potentially manipulate who called whom.$$),
('ci-f-1121','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$How does Virtual Desktop Infrastructure (VDI) reduce data-exfiltration risk compared to a traditional remote-access VPN?$$,$$With VDI, the user's actual desktop, applications, and data all run on a server in the datacenter; the remote device only receives screen images and sends keyboard/mouse input, so corporate data never actually leaves the datacenter to reside on the endpoint. A traditional VPN, by contrast, typically lets data be downloaded to and processed on the remote device itself, widening the surface for local data loss if that device is lost, stolen, or compromised.$$),
('ci-f-1122','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$What is the difference between an always-on VPN and an on-demand (manual) VPN?$$,$$An always-on VPN automatically establishes and maintains the tunnel whenever the device has network connectivity, without requiring the user to manually connect, ensuring traffic is consistently protected and policy is consistently enforced. An on-demand VPN requires the user to manually initiate the connection (or triggers only for specific destinations), leaving gaps where traffic can flow unprotected before the user connects.$$),
('ci-f-1123','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$What role does a Network Access Control (NAC) posture check play before granting remote-access VPN or ZTNA access?$$,$$A posture check verifies the connecting device meets security requirements - up-to-date patches, active endpoint protection, disk encryption enabled, no known malware - before granting access, and can quarantine or restrict a non-compliant device to a remediation network instead of full access, preventing a compromised or under-maintained endpoint from becoming the entry point into the corporate network.$$),
('ci-f-1124','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$What is VoIP toll fraud, and how does unencrypted SIP signaling enable it?$$,$$Toll fraud is when an attacker gains unauthorized use of a VoIP system to place calls (often to premium-rate international numbers) at the victim organization's expense. Unencrypted SIP signaling can expose authentication credentials or session details in transit, letting an attacker capture them and register as a legitimate endpoint or hijack an existing session to place fraudulent calls.$$),
-- 4.3 acronym (ci-a-374..375)
('ci-a-374','cissp','acronym',4,'4.3',$$ZTNA$$,$$Zero Trust Network Access - a remote-access model that grants per-application access after continuous identity/device verification, rather than placing the remote device on the internal network the way a traditional VPN does.$$),
('ci-a-375','cissp','acronym',4,'4.3',$$VDI$$,$$Virtual Desktop Infrastructure - hosting a full desktop environment on a central server and streaming only screen/input to the remote device, keeping corporate data off the endpoint.$$),
-- 4.3 feynman (ci-y-233..234)
('ci-y-233','cissp','feynman',4,'4.3',$$Explain why split tunneling is a genuine security trade-off rather than something to reflexively enable or disable.$$,$$Model answer: Split tunneling sends only corporate-destined traffic through the VPN while everything else - streaming video, personal browsing, SaaS traffic - exits directly from the user's local connection. That's a real, legitimate win: it cuts VPN bandwidth and infrastructure cost, and it reduces latency for traffic that never needed to detour through corporate infrastructure in the first place. The cost is equally real: none of that non-corporate traffic passes through corporate proxies, content filtering, DLP, or IDS/IPS, so if the user's device is compromised through that unfiltered path, or credentials are phished over it, the attacker can potentially pivot toward the corporate tunnel from an already-compromised device. Whether split tunneling is the right call depends on what's actually reachable once inside the tunnel (a flat network makes the risk far worse than a tightly segmented one), the endpoint's own security posture (EDR, patching), and what data sensitivity is at stake - it isn't simply "insecure," it is a bandwidth-versus-inspection trade that has to be weighed deliberately, not defaulted into.$$),
('ci-y-234','cissp','feynman',4,'4.3',$$Explain why exposing RDP directly to the internet is dangerous and what layered controls should wrap it instead.$$,$$Model answer: A bare, internet-facing RDP port is directly reachable by anyone worldwide, and automated scanners find and continuously hammer it with credential-stuffing and brute-force login attempts within minutes of it appearing - RDP has repeatedly been documented as a leading initial-access vector for ransomware precisely because it offers a full interactive desktop session if the attacker guesses right even once, and because pre-authentication vulnerabilities in the RDP stack itself have been exploited directly. The fix is never to expose port 3389 to the raw internet at all: put a VPN (with its own MFA) in front of it so RDP is only reachable from an already-authenticated tunnel, or route through a dedicated Remote Desktop Gateway, and require NLA plus strong/MFA-backed credentials on the RDP session itself as a second layer - each control assumes the one before it might fail, rather than relying on RDP's own login screen as the sole barrier.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 4.1 secure network architecture and protocols (ci-q-1100..1106)
('ci-q-1100','cissp',4,'4.1','ordering',1,$q$Order the three steps of the TCP three-way handshake used to establish a connection, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Client sends a SYN segment proposing an initial sequence number","Server responds with a SYN-ACK segment acknowledging the client and proposing its own sequence number","Client sends an ACK segment acknowledging the server's sequence number, completing the connection"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$The handshake begins with the client's SYN, the server's SYN-ACK combining its own SYN with an acknowledgment of the client's, and finally the client's ACK acknowledging the server - only after all three complete does either side consider the connection established, which is exactly the state a stateful firewall tracks to distinguish legitimate return traffic from unsolicited packets.$q$),
('ci-q-1101','cissp',4,'4.1','matching',2,$q$Match each protocol to the OSI layer at which it primarily operates.$q$,$q${"left":["ARP","IP","TCP","HTTP"],"right":["Transport (Layer 4) - provides connection-oriented, reliable, ordered delivery","Application (Layer 7) - the user-facing protocol carrying the actual request/response content","Data Link (Layer 2) - resolves an IP address to a local hardware/MAC address","Network (Layer 3) - handles logical addressing and routing between networks"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$ARP resolves addresses at Layer 2 within a local segment; IP handles Layer 3 routing between networks; TCP provides Layer 4 reliable delivery; HTTP is the Layer 7 application protocol actually carrying a request's content - each protocol's layer determines what information it can see and what a security control operating at that layer can inspect or filter.$q$),
('ci-q-1102','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary purpose of IKE Phase 1 when two gateways establish an IPsec VPN?$q$,$q$["To establish a protected management channel between the peers, over which the actual IPsec security associations will later be negotiated","To encrypt and transmit the actual application data between the two networks","To assign IP addresses to remote-access VPN clients","To generate the digital certificate used to authenticate a web server"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$IKE Phase 1 authenticates the two peers and negotiates a protected ISAKMP/IKE channel first; only in Phase 2, conducted inside that protected channel, are the actual IPsec SAs (the AH/ESP parameters that will protect application data) negotiated. Application data itself is never carried inside IKE - it flows through the IPsec tunnel established as a result of these negotiations.$q$),
('ci-q-1103','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',1,$q$A company wants each employee's Wi-Fi access tied to their own individual credentials, so that revoking one departing employee's access doesn't require changing a shared network password for everyone else. Which wireless authentication mode should it deploy?$q$,$q$["WPA2-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication against a RADIUS server","WPA2-Personal with a single shared pre-shared key","An open network with a captive portal only","WEP with per-device static keys"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$WPA2-Enterprise authenticates each user individually through 802.1X against a RADIUS server (or similar), so access can be revoked per-account without touching anyone else's credentials. WPA2-Personal's single shared PSK requires a full network-wide passphrase rotation to fully revoke one person's access; open networks and WEP offer far weaker security guarantees entirely.$q$),
('ci-q-1104','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',3,$q$An attacker connected to a switch port sends frames carrying two stacked 802.1Q VLAN tags. The switch strips the outer tag - matching its native VLAN - and forwards the frame, exposing the inner tag to a second switch that then delivers it into a VLAN the attacker was never granted access to. What attack and mitigation does this describe?$q$,$q$["VLAN hopping via double tagging, mitigated by never carrying user traffic on the native VLAN and explicitly tagging or disabling the native VLAN on trunk ports","ARP spoofing, mitigated by enabling dynamic ARP inspection","A VLAN hopping attack that cannot be mitigated because 802.1Q has no defense against it","DNS cache poisoning, mitigated by enabling DNSSEC on the switch"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Double-tagging VLAN hopping exploits how trunk ports strip the outer tag when it matches the native VLAN, letting a crafted inner tag reach an unauthorized VLAN. The standard mitigation is to never use the native/default VLAN for actual user traffic and to explicitly tag or disable it on trunk links, closing the gap the attack depends on. ARP spoofing and DNS poisoning are distinct attacks addressed by different controls.$q$),
('ci-q-1105','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Why is SNMPv3 considered a significant security improvement over SNMPv1 and SNMPv2c for managing network devices?$q$,$q$["SNMPv3 adds the User-based Security Model, providing authentication and encryption, whereas v1/v2c rely on cleartext community strings for access control","SNMPv3 uses a faster transport protocol that reduces polling latency","SNMPv3 eliminates the need for any access control on managed devices","SNMPv1/v2c already encrypt all traffic, so SNMPv3 only adds new monitoring features"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SNMPv1 and v2c authenticate and authorize access using "community strings" sent in cleartext, effectively a shared password anyone sniffing the network can capture. SNMPv3 introduces the User-based Security Model (USM), adding real per-user authentication and optional encryption of the SNMP traffic itself - the actual security upgrade, not a performance change.$q$),
('ci-q-1106','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$An organization configures its iSCSI initiators and targets to require CHAP authentication before establishing a storage session. What does this primarily protect against?$q$,$q$["An unauthorized host connecting to and reading or writing an iSCSI target's storage volumes","Physical theft of the storage array's disks","Data corruption from a failed RAID rebuild","Loss of data in transit due to network congestion"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$CHAP (Challenge Handshake Authentication Protocol) on iSCSI requires both the initiator and, in mutual CHAP, the target to prove knowledge of a shared secret before a storage session is established, preventing an unauthorized host on the same network from simply connecting to and accessing an iSCSI target's volumes. It addresses authentication, not physical security, RAID reliability, or network performance.$q$),
-- 4.2 secure network components (ci-q-1107..1116)
('ci-q-1107','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',1,$q$How does an application-layer (proxy) firewall differ from a simple packet-filtering firewall in what it can enforce?$q$,$q$["It terminates the connection itself and can inspect and act on the actual application content (e.g., an HTTP request's URL or headers), not just header fields","It only examines the source and destination IP address of each packet","It cannot examine any Layer 7 protocol content under any circumstances","It operates exclusively at Layer 1, inspecting only electrical signal patterns"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A proxy firewall terminates the client connection, inspects and can modify the actual application-layer content, and only then relays a new connection to the destination - giving it visibility a packet filter (which only checks packet headers) never has. This lets it enforce content-level policy, like blocking specific URLs or file types, that header inspection alone cannot.$q$),
('ci-q-1108','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$A company places its public-facing web server between two firewalls, in a buffer network isolated from the internal corporate network. What is this architecture called, and what does it primarily protect?$q$,$q$["A screened subnet (DMZ), which limits how far an attacker who compromises the public-facing server can reach into the internal network","A honeynet, which exists solely to be attacked and provides no production functionality","A circuit-level gateway, which verifies session handshakes without inspecting payload","A bastion host, which is itself the name for the buffer network rather than a server within it"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A screened subnet (DMZ) is exactly this buffer-network design: public-facing servers sit between two firewalls (or on a third interface) so that compromising one still leaves the attacker facing another firewall before reaching internal systems. A bastion host is a hardened server that may live inside such a DMZ, not the DMZ itself; a honeynet and circuit-level gateway describe unrelated concepts.$q$),
('ci-q-1109','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',1,$q$What best describes a bastion host?$q$,$q$["A hardened, minimally configured server intentionally exposed to untrusted networks because it is expected to face direct attack","A firewall rule that always permits traffic regardless of source","A backup server kept offline and unreachable from any network","A device that exists only to decrypt VPN traffic"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A bastion host is deliberately stripped down and hardened - unnecessary services disabled, patched aggressively, closely monitored - because its exposed position (often in a DMZ) makes it a direct and expected attack target; the goal is to minimize what an attacker gains even if they do compromise it.$q$),
('ci-q-1110','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$A security team wants visibility into file integrity changes and local process activity on a specific critical database server, even for activity that never traverses the network in cleartext. Which detection approach best fits this requirement?$q$,$q$["A host-based IDS (HIDS) agent installed on that server","A network-based IDS (NIDS) monitoring a mirrored switch port","A firewall access control list applied at the network edge","A vulnerability scanner run once per quarter"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A HIDS runs directly on the host, giving it visibility into local file integrity, process activity, and system calls regardless of whether that activity ever appears on the network or was encrypted in transit. A NIDS only sees traffic crossing the network segment it monitors and loses visibility once traffic is encrypted end-to-end or never leaves the host at all.$q$),
('ci-q-1111','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$A security architect is deciding whether to deploy a new detection appliance inline with production traffic (as an IPS) or passively via a mirrored SPAN port (as an IDS). What is the central trade-off between these two choices?$q$,$q$["Inline deployment can actively block malicious traffic but adds latency and a potential single point of failure; passive deployment adds no latency or failure risk but can only alert, never block","Passive deployment can block traffic in real time while inline deployment can only alert after the fact","There is no meaningful difference between the two deployment modes","Inline deployment is only usable for host-based detection, never network-based detection"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Placing detection directly in the traffic path lets it drop malicious packets before they reach their destination, but that same position adds processing latency and turns the device into a potential bottleneck or outage point if it fails. A passive tap/SPAN deployment only ever receives a copy of traffic, so it cannot interfere with the live path at all, but for the same reason it can never block anything itself.$q$),
('ci-q-1112','cissp',4,'4.2','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements that correctly describe anomaly-based (behavioral) detection compared to signature-based detection.$q$,$q$["Anomaly-based detection can identify previously unseen, novel attacks that have no existing signature","Anomaly-based detection generally has a higher false-positive rate than mature signature-based detection","Signature-based detection is effective against zero-day attacks with no prior signature","Anomaly-based detection requires no baseline of normal behavior to function"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Because anomaly-based detection flags deviations from a learned baseline of normal behavior rather than matching known patterns, it can catch genuinely novel attacks, but that same sensitivity to "unusual" activity tends to generate more false positives than a mature signature library tuned against known threats. Signature-based detection is, by definition, blind to zero-days with no matching signature yet, and anomaly-based detection depends entirely on first building an accurate baseline.$q$),
('ci-q-1113','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$An attacker sends a crafted HTTP request containing a SQL injection payload to a company's public web application. The company's network firewall, which filters by IP address and port, does not block it. What control is specifically designed to catch this kind of attack?$q$,$q$["A Web Application Firewall (WAF) inspecting HTTP(S) request content","A circuit-level gateway verifying the TCP handshake","A packet-filtering firewall blocking additional source IPs","Increasing the network firewall's maximum connection table size"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A WAF inspects the actual content of HTTP(S) requests - query strings, headers, body - against rulesets designed to catch application-layer attacks like SQL injection and cross-site scripting. A network-layer firewall filtering by IP/port has no visibility into that request content at all, so a well-formed request to an allowed port on an allowed IP sails through regardless of what its payload contains.$q$),
('ci-q-1114','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',3,$q$A laptop attempts to connect to the corporate network but is found to be missing recent security patches. Network Access Control (NAC) redirects it to an isolated remediation VLAN instead of granting full network access. What is this behavior an example of?$q$,$q$["Posture/health assessment gating network admission based on device compliance","A firewall implicit-deny rule blocking all traffic by default","A honeypot isolating a suspected attacker","A stateful firewall dropping out-of-state packets"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NAC performs a posture (health) check against policy - patch level, endpoint protection status, and similar criteria - before granting network access, and can quarantine a non-compliant device to a restricted remediation segment instead. This is distinct from a firewall's implicit-deny default rule, a honeypot's deception purpose, or stateful inspection's connection tracking.$q$),
('ci-q-1115','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',1,$q$A firewall administrator adds a new, narrow rule blocking a specific malicious IP address, but places it below an existing broad rule that allows all traffic from that IP's subnet. On a firewall that evaluates rules top-down and stops at the first match, what happens?$q$,$q$["The broad allow rule matches first, so the new block rule never takes effect","The narrow block rule always takes precedence regardless of its position","The firewall automatically reorders rules from most specific to least specific","Both rules apply simultaneously and the connection is blocked"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$On a first-match, top-down rule evaluation engine, whichever rule the traffic matches first wins, and evaluation stops there - a broad allow rule placed above a more specific deny rule silently shadows it, which is exactly why specific rules must be placed above general ones and rule-base review is a standard firewall hygiene practice.$q$),
('ci-q-1116','cissp',4,'4.2','matching',3,$q$Match each network security component to its primary function.$q$,$q${"left":["Bastion host","Screened subnet (DMZ)","Honeypot","Unified Threat Management (UTM) appliance"],"right":["A decoy system with no legitimate purpose, used to detect and study attacker activity","A single appliance combining firewall, IPS, antivirus, and content filtering into one integrated platform","A hardened server deliberately exposed to untrusted networks because it is expected to face direct attack","A buffer network between two firewalls that isolates public-facing servers from the internal network"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each of these plays a distinct architectural role: a bastion host absorbs direct exposure by design; a screened subnet contains public-facing services behind a second layer of defense; a honeypot exists purely as bait for detection and threat intelligence; and a UTM appliance consolidates several security functions - firewall, IPS, AV, content filtering - into a single managed device, trading some best-of-breed depth for simplified management.$q$),
-- 4.3 secure communication channels (ci-q-1117..1128)
('ci-q-1117','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',1,$q$A company wants remote contractors using personal, unmanaged devices to reach one specific internal web application without installing dedicated VPN client software. Which VPN approach best fits this need?$q$,$q$["A clientless SSL/TLS VPN accessed through a standard web browser","A site-to-site IPsec VPN between two network gateways","A full-tunnel IPsec VPN requiring a dedicated client on the device","WPA2-Enterprise wireless authentication"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Clientless SSL/TLS VPNs are delivered through an ordinary browser and can be scoped to expose just a specific application or portal, making them well-suited to unmanaged or personal devices where installing dedicated client software is undesirable. IPsec VPNs typically require a client and grant broader network-level access; a site-to-site VPN connects networks, not individual unmanaged devices; WPA2-Enterprise addresses wireless authentication, not remote application access.$q$),
('ci-q-1118','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A remote employee's VPN is configured so that every packet the laptop sends, including personal web browsing and streaming, routes through the corporate VPN tunnel and its security stack before reaching the internet. What is the primary trade-off of this full-tunnel configuration compared to split tunneling?$q$,$q$["It maximizes corporate visibility and inspection of all traffic, at the cost of added latency and VPN bandwidth load","It has no security benefit over split tunneling because DNS still resolves locally","It removes the need for any endpoint security software on the laptop","It automatically blocks all internet access other than corporate resources"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Full tunneling routes all traffic, corporate and personal alike, through the VPN and its inspection/filtering stack, giving the organization complete visibility at the cost of the added latency and bandwidth consumption of backhauling every connection - the inverse trade-off from split tunneling, which sacrifices that visibility for performance.$q$),
('ci-q-1119','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$Two branch offices need an always-available, transparent network connection between them that requires no end-user login or client software once configured. Which VPN type fits this requirement?$q$,$q$["A site-to-site VPN between the two offices' gateways","A remote-access (client-to-site) VPN for each employee","A clientless SSL VPN portal","Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA) per application"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A site-to-site VPN is an always-up tunnel established between two network gateways, transparently connecting entire networks with no per-user login or client involved - exactly what persistent branch-to-branch connectivity calls for. Remote-access VPN, clientless SSL VPN, and ZTNA are all designed around individual user sessions, not transparent network-to-network connectivity.$q$),
('ci-q-1120','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',3,$q$An organization replaces its traditional remote-access VPN with a solution that authenticates and authorizes each application request individually, never placing the remote device directly onto the internal network, and continuously re-evaluates device and identity context during the session. What is this approach called?$q$,$q$["Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA)","Split tunneling","A screened subnet","IKE Phase 1 negotiation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$ZTNA brokers access to individual applications based on continuously verified identity and device posture, rather than granting broad network-level access after a single login the way a traditional VPN does - the remote device never actually joins the internal network segment. Split tunneling, screened subnets, and IKE phases describe unrelated VPN/network concepts.$q$),
('ci-q-1121','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary security benefit of enabling Network Level Authentication (NLA) for Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)?$q$,$q$["It requires authentication to complete before a full remote session is established, reducing exposure to pre-authentication attacks and resource exhaustion","It encrypts the RDP session using SRTP","It replaces the need for a VPN when RDP is exposed to the internet","It automatically patches the RDP service"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NLA moves authentication earlier in the connection process, requiring credentials before the full remote desktop session stack is even initialized, which reduces the attack surface available to unauthenticated connection attempts and resource-exhaustion attacks. It does not use SRTP (a VoIP media protocol), replace the need for a VPN, or patch anything automatically.$q$),
('ci-q-1122','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A systems administrator opens port 3389 on the perimeter firewall so RDP is directly reachable from the internet, reasoning that a strong password is sufficient protection. What is the main risk with this approach?$q$,$q$["Internet-facing RDP is continuously targeted by automated credential-stuffing, brute-force, and exploit attempts, and has been a leading initial-access vector for ransomware","RDP cannot function at all unless directly exposed to the internet","Modern RDP encrypts all traffic so no additional protection is ever necessary","Port 3389 is reserved and cannot actually be opened on a standard firewall"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Directly exposed RDP is one of the most heavily and continuously scanned services on the internet, and attackers routinely succeed through credential stuffing, brute-forcing weak passwords, or exploiting RDP-stack vulnerabilities - documented repeatedly as a top initial-access vector for ransomware. Strong passwords alone don't address stack-level vulnerabilities or credential reuse from other breaches; best practice puts RDP behind a VPN or gateway rather than exposing it directly.$q$),
('ci-q-1123','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A VoIP deployment encrypts the voice media stream with SRTP but leaves SIP signaling traffic unencrypted. What remains exposed despite SRTP being in place?$q$,$q$["Call setup and signaling details, such as who is calling whom, and any credentials carried in the SIP exchange","The actual audio content of the phone calls","Nothing, because SRTP alone secures a VoIP deployment completely","The physical location of the VoIP server's data center"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SRTP protects only the media (audio) stream; SIP handles call signaling - setup, teardown, and metadata like caller/callee identity - and if left unencrypted (rather than run as SIPS over TLS), that signaling and any credentials or session details within it remain exposed to interception, even though the actual conversation audio is protected.$q$),
('ci-q-1124','cissp',4,'4.3','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO controls that together provide layered protection for a VoIP deployment against both eavesdropping and signaling manipulation.$q$,$q$["SRTP to encrypt the media (voice) stream","TLS (SIPS) to encrypt the SIP signaling traffic","Increasing the codec bitrate for better call quality","Disabling all call logging to reduce attacker interest"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$SRTP and SIPS address two different attack surfaces in a VoIP deployment - the actual voice content and the call setup/signaling metadata, respectively - and neither alone protects the other. Codec bitrate is a quality setting unrelated to security, and disabling logging removes an organization's own visibility rather than protecting against an attacker.$q$),
('ci-q-1125','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A company handling highly sensitive data wants contractors to work with that data from personal, unmanaged laptops without the data ever being downloaded to those devices. Which remote-access approach best achieves this?$q$,$q$["Virtual Desktop Infrastructure (VDI), streaming only screen images and input to the endpoint","A full-tunnel IPsec VPN client installed on the personal laptop","A split-tunnel VPN scoped to the sensitive data repository","Email-based file transfer with password-protected attachments"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$VDI keeps the actual desktop, applications, and data running on a server in the datacenter, sending only screen output to the remote device and receiving only keyboard/mouse input back - the data itself never has to leave the datacenter to reside on an unmanaged endpoint. Both VPN options still typically let data be downloaded to and processed on the device itself, and emailing attachments defeats the goal entirely.$q$),
('ci-q-1126','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',1,$q$What is the main benefit of an always-on VPN configuration compared to one the user must manually connect each time?$q$,$q$["It automatically establishes and maintains the protected tunnel whenever the device has connectivity, closing the gap where traffic could flow unprotected before the user remembers to connect","It uses less bandwidth than a manually initiated VPN connection","It eliminates the need for user authentication entirely","It only works with SSL/TLS VPNs, never IPsec VPNs"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Always-on VPN removes reliance on the user remembering (or choosing) to connect, automatically establishing and reconnecting the tunnel as soon as connectivity is available, so corporate policy and inspection apply consistently rather than only during whatever windows the user happened to manually connect. It doesn't reduce bandwidth use, eliminate authentication, or restrict which VPN technology can be used.$q$),
('ci-q-1127','cissp',4,'4.3','ordering',2,$q$Order the typical sequence of events when a remote-access VPN client with NAC posture checking connects to the corporate network.$q$,$q$["The device establishes network connectivity and initiates the VPN client connection","The user authenticates (e.g., with MFA) to the VPN gateway","NAC evaluates the device's posture (patch level, endpoint protection status) against policy","The VPN tunnel is fully established and routing (full or split tunnel) is applied according to policy"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A properly layered remote-access flow initiates the connection attempt first, then confirms the user's identity, then checks whether the device meets security policy before trusting it further, and only after all of that grants and configures the actual tunnel - if posture checking ran before authentication, or tunnel setup happened before either check, a non-compliant or unauthenticated device could gain access the sequence is specifically designed to prevent.$q$),
('ci-q-1128','cissp',4,'4.3','matching',3,$q$Match each remote-access or VoIP security term to its description.$q$,$q${"left":["SRTP","Split tunneling","Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA)","Network Level Authentication (NLA)"],"right":["Requires authentication to complete before a full RDP session is established","Grants per-application access after continuous verification instead of broad network-level trust","Encrypts the voice media stream in a VoIP call to prevent eavesdropping","Routes only corporate-destined traffic through the VPN tunnel while other traffic exits locally"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,1,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each term addresses a distinct part of secure remote communication: SRTP protects VoIP media confidentiality; split tunneling is a VPN routing trade-off between performance and inspection coverage; ZTNA replaces broad VPN network trust with per-application, continuously verified access; and NLA hardens RDP specifically by moving authentication earlier in the connection process.$q$);
